LZ.data.listening.push({id:"l1",title:"Listening Test 1",topics:"Everyday situations · A wildlife camerawoman · Why people run · A young cellist",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear a woman telling a friend about a cookery course.",q:"What did she like most about it?",o:["the chef's way of teaching","the recipes they cooked","meeting other people"],a:0,x:"‘What really made it for me was Marco, the chef. He never just told us what to do – he'd explain why.’ The recipes were only ‘fine’ and she didn't talk to many people.",script:[["M","So, how was the cookery course? You were really looking forward to it."],["F","It was great, actually. I thought the recipes might be a bit too ambitious for beginners, but they were fine – nothing I couldn't manage at home. And there was a nice mix of people, although to be honest I didn't really talk to many of them. What really made it for me was Marco, the chef. He never just told us what to do – he'd explain why, like why you add salt at a certain point. That's what's stuck with me."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about his journey to work.",q:"How does he feel about his new route?",o:["relieved that it is quicker","pleased that it is more interesting","annoyed that it costs more"],a:1,x:"It ‘adds about ten minutes’ (not quicker) and the fare is the same (not more expensive), but it has ‘become the best part of my day’.",script:[["M","Since they closed the bridge, I've had to go the long way round, through the old town. I was dreading it – it adds about ten minutes, and I thought I'd be stuck in traffic the whole time. But it's actually become the best part of my day. There's a market on Tuesdays, and I've started noticing all these lovely old buildings I never looked at before. The bus fare's exactly the same, so no complaints there. I might even keep going that way when the bridge reopens."]]},{ctx:"You hear two students talking about a school project.",q:"What do they agree to do?",o:["ask for more time to finish it","change the topic completely","get advice from their teacher"],a:2,x:"The boy says ‘Let's see what Mrs Evans thinks before we decide anything’ and the girl agrees: ‘Good idea.’ He rejects changing topic (‘starting from scratch’).",script:[["F","I'm starting to think we've chosen something far too big. There's just so much information about climate change."],["M","I know what you mean, but we've already done loads of research. Changing now would mean starting from scratch."],["F","True. Maybe we could just focus on one part of it – like how it affects farming?"],["M","Hmm, that's still quite broad. Let's see what Mrs Evans thinks before we decide anything."],["F","Good idea. She'll know whether it's manageable."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman leaving a phone message for a restaurant.",q:"What does she want to change?",o:["the number of people","the time of the booking","the day of the booking"],a:1,x:"‘It's still four of us’ and she mentions Saturday, but asks ‘would it be possible to make it a bit later – half past eight?’",script:[["F","Hi, this is Julia Reeves. I've got a table booked for four people on Saturday evening at eight o'clock. We're definitely still coming, and it's still four of us, but would it be possible to make it a bit later – half past eight, say? One of my friends is working until seven and she won't make it in time otherwise. If there's a problem, you can call me back on this number. Thanks very much."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a novel he has just read.",q:"What is his opinion of the book?",o:["The ending was disappointing.","The characters weren't convincing.","It was far too long."],a:0,x:"‘It's just a shame about the last chapter… everything got sorted out far too neatly.’ The characters ‘felt like real people’ and he didn't notice the length.",script:[["M","Everyone's been raving about it, so I had high expectations. And for the most part, it lived up to them. It's over six hundred pages, but I honestly didn't notice – I read it in a week. The characters felt like real people, especially the grandmother. It's just a shame about the last chapter. After all that build-up, everything got sorted out far too neatly, as if the writer had run out of ideas. It rather spoiled it for me."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about learning to drive.",q:"What does she say about her driving instructor?",o:["He was too critical of her.","He made her feel more confident.","He was often late for lessons."],a:1,x:"He pointed out mistakes ‘in a way that made me think I could fix them’, and ‘by the end I actually looked forward to lessons’. He ‘was never late’.",script:[["F","I was terrified before my first lesson – I'd heard all these stories about instructors shouting. But Ken was the calmest person I've ever met. Even when I stalled at a roundabout three times, he just said, ‘No rush, try again.’ He did point out my mistakes, obviously, but always in a way that made me think I could fix them. By the end I actually looked forward to lessons. And he was never late, not once."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man and a woman talking about a new sports centre.",q:"What does the man think is the main problem with it?",o:["the equipment in the gym","its location","the cost of membership"],a:2,x:"‘Sixty pounds a month! … I just can't justify it.’ He praises the equipment, and the location ‘doesn't bother me’.",script:[["F","Have you been to the new sports centre yet?"],["M","Yeah, I went on Sunday. The pool's fantastic, and the gym has all the latest equipment."],["F","So you'll be joining, then?"],["M","I'd love to, but have you seen the prices? Sixty pounds a month! The old place was half that. The facilities are great, but I just can't justify it."],["F","And it's miles out of town."],["M","Well, that doesn't bother me – there's a bus stop right outside."]]},{ctx:"You hear a tour guide speaking to a group of tourists.",q:"What is the guide doing?",o:["describing the history of a building","warning the group about a danger","explaining a change to the programme"],a:2,x:"The castle is closed, ‘so instead, we'll be going to the maritime museum’. The storm is mentioned, but nobody is in danger now.",script:[["F","Right, everyone, can I have your attention for a moment? I know we were supposed to visit the castle this afternoon, but they've just closed it for repairs – part of the roof came down in last night's storm. Nobody was hurt, luckily. So instead, we'll be going to the maritime museum, which is just as interesting, I promise, and then you'll have a bit more free time in the old town before dinner."]]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a woman called Laura Finch talking about her work filming wildlife for television.",script:[["F",`Hello everyone, and thanks for inviting me. I'm Laura Finch, and for the last twelve years I've been filming wildlife for television documentaries. People often ask how I got started. Well, I grew up on a farm, so I was always surrounded by animals, but it was when my uncle gave me his old video camera for my thirteenth birthday that I really got hooked. I spent that whole summer filming the swallows nesting in our barn.

After university, where I studied zoology rather than film, which surprises a lot of people, I got my first job as an assistant on a series about sharks. I thought it would be glamorous, but most of my time was spent carrying heavy equipment and cleaning lenses.

The thing most people don't realise about this job is how much waiting is involved. For one series, I spent forty-three days in a hide in the Scottish Highlands, waiting for a golden eagle to return to its nest. People imagine it's the cold that's hardest, but actually it's the boredom.

Of course, there are dangerous moments too. The scariest experience I've had wasn't with a big predator, as you might expect. It was in Borneo, when a swarm of hornets attacked our camp. I was stung eleven times!

Technology has changed the job enormously. When I started, we used big, heavy cameras, but now we often use tiny ones that can be hidden inside fake rocks. Drones have also been a huge help, particularly for filming herds of animals from above without disturbing them.

My favourite project so far was a film about otters living in a city river in Norway. What made it special was that local schoolchildren helped us by recording where they'd seen the otters each day.

If you're thinking of doing this kind of work, my main piece of advice is to start small. You don't need to go to the Arctic. Film the birds in your garden or the insects in your local park, and learn everything you can about their behaviour. The best wildlife filmmakers are really naturalists first. And put your films online – that's how I got noticed.`]],items:[{s:"Laura was given her first video camera by her {g} when she was thirteen.",a:["uncle"],x:"‘my uncle gave me his old video camera for my thirteenth birthday’"},{s:"At university, Laura studied {g}.",a:["zoology"],x:"‘I studied zoology rather than film’ – film is the distractor."},{s:"In her first job, Laura mostly carried {g} and cleaned lenses.",a:["heavy equipment","equipment"],x:"‘most of my time was spent carrying heavy equipment and cleaning lenses’"},{s:"Laura once waited in a hide in Scotland for {g} days.",a:["forty-three","43","forty three"],x:"‘I spent forty-three days in a hide’"},{s:"Laura says the hardest part of waiting is not the cold but the {g}.",a:["boredom"],x:"‘People imagine it's the cold that's hardest, but actually it's the boredom.’"},{s:"Laura's most frightening experience involved {g} in Borneo.",a:["hornets","a swarm of hornets"],x:"‘It wasn't with a big predator… a swarm of hornets attacked our camp.’"},{s:"{g} are very useful for filming herds without disturbing them.",a:["drones"],x:"‘Drones have also been a huge help, particularly for filming herds of animals from above’"},{s:"Laura's favourite film was about {g} in a city river.",a:["otters"],x:"‘a film about otters living in a city river in Norway’"},{s:"Local {g} helped the team by recording where the animals were seen.",a:["schoolchildren","school children","schoolkids"],x:"‘local schoolchildren helped us by recording where they'd seen the otters’"},{s:"Laura believes the best wildlife filmmakers are {g} first.",a:["naturalists"],x:"‘The best wildlife filmmakers are really naturalists first.’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people are talking about why they started running.",prompt:"For questions 19–23, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker says about starting to run. Use the letters only once. There are three extra letters which you do not need to use.",options:["I was following a doctor's advice.","I wanted to spend less money on transport.","I did it to raise money for a charity.","I needed a way to cope with stress.","I was persuaded by a relative.","I wanted to prove someone wrong.","I wanted to meet new people.","I was inspired by watching a race."],speakers:[{a:4,x:"E: her brother ‘kept going on at me to join him… I gave in’. The doctor is pleased but ‘never actually suggested it’ (A).",script:[["F","I'd never been sporty at school – I was always the last one picked for teams. Then last year my brother, who's been running for ages, kept going on at me to join him on Saturday mornings. I said no about fifty times. In the end I gave in just to keep him quiet, and I'm glad I did. We run together every weekend now, and it's brought us closer. My doctor's delighted too, though she never actually suggested it."]]},{a:3,x:"D: his job is ‘high-pressure’ and running is ‘my way of dealing with a difficult day’. Saving on train fares is ‘just a bonus’ (B).",script:[["M","My job in sales is quite high-pressure, and a couple of years ago I wasn't sleeping well at all. A colleague mentioned that going for a run after work helped her switch off. I was sceptical, but I tried it, and honestly, within a week I felt calmer. It's become my way of dealing with a difficult day. I've even started running to work sometimes, which saves me a bit on train fares, but that's just a bonus."]]},{a:7,x:"H: watching people cross the marathon finish line made her think ‘I want to feel that’. Raising money came later (C).",script:[["F","A few years ago I went to watch my cousin in the city marathon. I stood at the finish line for hours, and seeing all these ordinary people – some of them in their seventies – crossing the line in tears, I just thought, ‘I want to feel that.’ I signed up for my first race the next week. I've done three marathons since then, and I've raised money for a children's hospital along the way, which is lovely."]]},{a:6,x:"G: he ‘didn't know a soul’ and joined a club; ‘most of my closest friends here are people I met through the club’.",script:[["M","When I moved to a new city for work, I didn't know a soul, and evenings in my flat were pretty lonely. I saw a poster for a running club that met on Thursdays, and even though I wasn't particularly fit, I went along. Everyone was really welcoming, and we always go for a meal afterwards. Most of my closest friends here are people I met through the club. The running is almost secondary!"]]},{a:5,x:"F: her ex-boyfriend said she'd never have the discipline; she ran a half marathon and sent him the photo.",script:[["F","My ex-boyfriend once told me I'd never have the discipline to run even five kilometres. He said it as a joke, but it really annoyed me. So the day after we broke up, I downloaded a training app and started. Six months later I ran a half marathon, and I sent him the photo of me at the finish line. Childish, maybe, but very satisfying."]]}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a young musician called Ben Carter, who plays the cello in an orchestra.",script:[["F","My guest today is Ben Carter, who at just twenty-two has become one of the youngest members of the National Symphony Orchestra. Ben, welcome."],["M","Thanks for having me."],["F","You started playing the cello quite late, didn't you?"],["M","Yes, I was twelve, which is late for a classical musician. Most of my colleagues started when they were four or five. Actually, I wanted to play the drums, but my parents said our flat was too small and the neighbours would complain. The school had a spare cello nobody was using, so my music teacher suggested I give it a try. I wasn't keen at first, to be honest – it seemed a bit old-fashioned."],["F","So what changed your mind?"],["M","It was one particular piece of music. My teacher played me a recording of Bach's cello suites, and something about the sound – it's so close to a human voice – just grabbed me. I went home and practised for three hours. My parents couldn't believe it, because I'd never stuck at anything before."],["F","Getting into a professional orchestra is extremely competitive. How did you prepare for the audition?"],["M","The playing itself I'd been preparing for years. What I hadn't prepared for was the nerves. In the first audition I did, for a different orchestra, my hands were shaking so much that I couldn't control the bow, and I didn't get in. So before the next one, I performed in front of people as often as I possibly could – in care homes, at train stations, anywhere. By the time the real audition came, playing to a panel didn't feel so strange."],["F","What's life in an orchestra actually like?"],["M","People imagine it's all glamorous concerts, but there's a huge amount of rehearsal – often six hours a day – and a lot of travel. The hardest adjustment for me, though, was that you're no longer an individual. As a student, I was used to deciding how I wanted to play a piece. In an orchestra, the conductor decides, and you have to blend in with sixty other people. It took me a while to accept that."],["F","You've also become quite well known online."],["M","That was an accident, really. A friend filmed me playing a pop song on the cello in the street and put it on social media, and it got two million views in a week. Some people in the classical world were a bit sniffy about it, but I think it's brilliant if it gets young people interested. I get messages from kids saying they've started lessons because of my videos, and that means far more to me than the number of views."],["F","Do you ever find time to relax?"],["M","Not as much as I'd like. When I do, I go climbing. My colleagues think I'm mad – if I injured my hands, I couldn't play. But I'm careful, and I need something that gets me completely out of my head. When you're halfway up a rock face, you can't think about rehearsals or anything else."],["F","And finally, what's next for you?"],["M","I'd love to try composing. I've written a few short pieces, nothing serious yet. Eventually I'd like to write music for films. But I'm in no hurry – I've got a lot to learn from the orchestra first."]],qs:[{q:"Why did Ben start playing the cello?",o:["His parents wanted him to learn a classical instrument.","He wasn't able to play the instrument he wanted.","He was inspired by a performance at school."],a:1,x:"He wanted the drums, but the flat was too small, so his teacher suggested the spare cello."},{q:"What made Ben enthusiastic about the cello?",o:["the sound of a piece of music","his teacher's praise","his parents' reaction"],a:0,x:"Bach's cello suites – ‘something about the sound… just grabbed me’."},{q:"What did Ben do differently before his successful audition?",o:["He practised the pieces for longer.","He got used to playing in front of people.","He chose easier music to play."],a:1,x:"He ‘performed in front of people as often as I possibly could’ to deal with nerves."},{q:"What did Ben find hardest when he joined the orchestra?",o:["the long rehearsals","travelling so often","not being able to play in his own way"],a:2,x:"‘The hardest adjustment… the conductor decides, and you have to blend in.’"},{q:"How does Ben feel about his online popularity?",o:["proud that it has encouraged others to play","worried that it has damaged his reputation","surprised that other musicians approve of it"],a:0,x:"Messages from kids who started lessons ‘mean far more to me than the number of views’."},{q:"Why does Ben go climbing?",o:["to keep his hands strong","because his colleagues recommended it","because it stops him thinking about work"],a:2,x:"‘I need something that gets me completely out of my head… you can't think about rehearsals.’"},{q:"What does Ben say about his future?",o:["He plans to leave the orchestra soon.","He wants more experience before composing seriously.","He has already written music for a film."],a:1,x:"‘I'm in no hurry – I've got a lot to learn from the orchestra first.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.listening.push({id:"l2",title:"Listening Test 2",topics:"Everyday situations · A turtle volunteer · Moving home · The science of happiness",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear a man telling a friend about his new phone.",q:"What does he dislike about it?",o:["the battery life","the size of the screen","the cost of extras"],a:2,x:"‘What really gets me is that nothing's included any more… the case they recommend costs almost as much as a meal out.’ The battery ‘easily gets me through the day’ and he got used to the screen.",script:[["F","So, are you happy with the new phone?"],["M","Mostly, yes. The camera's amazing, and I was worried the battery wouldn't last, but it easily gets me through the day. The screen's a bit smaller than my old one, but I got used to that in about a week. What really gets me is that nothing's included any more. No charger, no headphones, and the case they recommend costs almost as much as a meal out. It feels like they're making money out of every little thing."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a course she did.",q:"Why did she decide to do the course?",o:["Her employer asked her to.","She was thinking of changing career.","A friend recommended it."],a:1,x:"‘I'd been thinking about leaving my job in marketing… I wanted to see whether I could actually earn a living taking photos.’ She paid herself, and her friend thought she was crazy.",script:[["F","People assume my company sent me on the photography course, because I use a camera at work sometimes, but actually I paid for it myself. I'd been thinking about leaving my job in marketing for a while, and I wanted to see whether I could actually earn a living taking photos. My friend Sarah thought I was crazy – she said I should just keep it as a hobby. Anyway, it went well, and I've got my first paid job next month."]]},{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about a restaurant.",q:"What do they agree about?",o:["The service was slow.","The food was good value.","There weren't enough choices on the menu."],a:1,x:"He says it was ‘really reasonable for the quality’ and she replies ‘Oh, definitely.’ They disagree about the waiting time and the vegetarian options.",script:[["F","I thought the food was great, but we waited forever between courses."],["M","Did we? I didn't really notice – we were chatting. What I did think was that it was really reasonable for the quality."],["F","Oh, definitely. I've paid twice as much for worse food."],["M","Me too. I'd have liked a few more vegetarian options, though."],["F","Really? I thought there was plenty."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man leaving a phone message for a colleague.",q:"What does he want her to do?",o:["email him a document","change the time of a meeting","book a meeting room"],a:0,x:"‘Could you email me the report I left on my desk?’ He doesn't want to move the meeting, and the room is already booked.",script:[["M","Hi Laura, it's Paul. I'm stuck on the train – there's a problem with the signals, so I'm going to be at least an hour late. The meeting with the clients is at ten, and I really don't want to move it again, so could you email me the report I left on my desk? Then I can read it on the way and be ready as soon as I arrive. The room's already booked, so don't worry about that. Thanks."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a holiday.",q:"How did she feel at the end of the holiday?",o:["sorry to be going home","disappointed by the weather","relieved to be leaving"],a:0,x:"‘When the taxi came to take us to the airport, I could have cried. I'm already looking at dates to go back.’ The rain they expected never came.",script:[["F","We'd been told it would rain the whole week, so we packed umbrellas and waterproofs, and in the end we didn't need any of them. The hotel was lovely and the town was so friendly. By the last day I'd completely switched off from work, and honestly, when the taxi came to take us to the airport, I could have cried. I'm already looking at dates to go back."]]},{ctx:"You hear a student talking about a school science project.",q:"What was the most difficult part of the project?",o:["collecting the data","working in a team","writing the report"],a:2,x:"‘The nightmare was putting it all into the final report… We rewrote it about five times.’ Getting data was ‘fun’ and the group ‘got on really well’.",script:[["M","Our project was on air quality around the school. Getting the data was actually fun – we had sensors all over the place, and everyone wanted to help. And the four of us in the group got on really well, which isn't always the case. The nightmare was putting it all into the final report. We had so much information, and we had to fit it into two thousand words. We rewrote it about five times."]]},{ctx:"You hear a gardening expert speaking on the radio.",q:"What is she doing?",o:["explaining why a method is popular","warning listeners about a mistake","describing her own garden"],a:1,x:"Watering a little every day ‘feels like the caring thing to do. But it actually keeps the roots near the surface’ – she warns against it and gives better advice.",script:[["F","A lot of you have written in about your tomato plants this summer, and I think I know what's going on. Many people are watering them a little every day, which feels like the caring thing to do. But it actually keeps the roots near the surface, and the plants suffer as soon as it gets hot. Water them deeply twice a week instead, and you'll see a real difference."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about learning to swim as an adult.",q:"What does he say about his swimming teacher?",o:["She made the lessons fun.","She was very strict.","She understood his fears."],a:2,x:"‘She'd clearly worked with plenty of nervous adults, and she never once pushed me to do something before I was ready.’ The lessons were serious, not fun (A), but she wasn't strict (B).",script:[["M","I was forty-two and couldn't swim, and I was so embarrassed about it that I nearly didn't turn up. The teacher, Carol, wasn't exactly a barrel of laughs – the lessons were serious and there was a lot of repetition. But she'd clearly worked with plenty of nervous adults, and she never once pushed me to do something before I was ready. I don't think I'd have kept going with anyone else."]]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a man called Marcus Webb talking about volunteering on a sea turtle project in Costa Rica.",script:[["M",`Hi everyone. I'm Marcus, and last year I spent four months volunteering on a sea turtle project on the Pacific coast of Costa Rica. I'd been working in an office in Manchester for six years, and I wanted to do something completely different before I turned thirty.

The project I joined protects the nests of olive ridley turtles. Every night, groups of volunteers walked along an eight-kilometre stretch of beach, looking for female turtles coming ashore to lay their eggs. We couldn't use normal torches, because bright light confuses the turtles, so we used red lights instead, which the turtles can't really see.

When we found a nest, we carefully collected the eggs and moved them to a protected area called a hatchery. That sounds strange, but the main threat to the eggs isn't other animals – it's poachers, people who dig up the eggs to sell them. In the hatchery, the eggs were guarded twenty-four hours a day.

The eggs take about forty-five days to hatch. My favourite job was releasing the baby turtles. We'd wait until the evening, when it was cooler, and let them walk down to the sea by themselves. It's important that they make that journey on their own, because it helps them remember the beach and return there as adults.

Life at the camp was basic. We slept in wooden huts, and there was electricity for only three hours a day, so I read more books in those four months than in the previous five years. The biggest challenge for me, though, wasn't the lack of comfort – it was the mosquitoes. I've never been bitten so much in my life.

Most volunteers were in their twenties, but there was a retired teacher from Canada who was seventy-one and could walk further than any of us. Everyone paid a weekly fee to cover food and accommodation, which surprised some people, but it also pays the local staff who run the project all year round.

If you're thinking of doing something similar, my advice is to check that the project works closely with the local community. The best projects employ local people and support schools in the area, so that protecting the turtles benefits everyone.

Since I came back, I've changed jobs, and I now work for an environmental charity. So in a way, the turtles changed my life as much as I helped theirs.`]],items:[{s:"Before the trip, Marcus had worked in an {g} for six years.",a:["office"],x:"‘I'd been working in an office in Manchester for six years.’"},{s:"Volunteers patrolled a beach that was {g} kilometres long.",a:["eight","8"],x:"‘an eight-kilometre stretch of beach’"},{s:"Volunteers used {g} lights so that the turtles wouldn't be confused.",a:["red"],x:"‘We couldn't use normal torches… so we used red lights instead.’"},{s:"Eggs were moved to a protected area known as a {g}.",a:["hatchery"],x:"‘a protected area called a hatchery’"},{s:"The main threat to the eggs comes from {g}.",a:["poachers"],x:"‘The main threat… isn't other animals – it's poachers.’"},{s:"The eggs hatch after about {g} days.",a:["forty-five","45","forty five"],x:"‘The eggs take about forty-five days to hatch.’"},{s:"Baby turtles were released in the {g}, when it was cooler.",a:["evening"],x:"‘We'd wait until the evening, when it was cooler.’"},{s:"At the camp there was electricity for {g} hours a day.",a:["three","3"],x:"‘electricity for only three hours a day’"},{s:"Marcus found the {g} the biggest challenge of camp life.",a:["mosquitoes","mosquitos"],x:"‘The biggest challenge… wasn't the lack of comfort – it was the mosquitoes.’"},{s:"Marcus now works for an {g}.",a:["environmental charity"],x:"‘I now work for an environmental charity.’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people are talking about moving to a new home.",prompt:"For questions 19–23, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker says about their move. Use the letters only once. There are three extra letters which you do not need to use.",options:["It took longer than I expected to feel at home.","The neighbours have been very welcoming.","I miss the place where I used to live.","It has improved my relationship with my family.","I underestimated how much the move would cost.","I've taken up a new activity since moving.","I had no choice about moving.","We made the decision too quickly."],speakers:[{a:0,x:"A: ‘for the first six months I felt like a tourist rather than someone who lived there… It was only around Christmas… that it started to feel like home.’",script:[["F","We moved to the coast last spring, and I'd imagined it would all be wonderful straight away. In reality, for the first six months I felt like a tourist rather than someone who lived there. I didn't know where anything was, and I kept driving back to the city at weekends to see old friends. It was only around Christmas, when I realised I knew the names of half the people in the local café, that it started to feel like home."]]},{a:4,x:"E: ‘By the end we'd spent nearly twice what we'd planned.’ The neighbours are only ‘nice enough’ (B).",script:[["M","Honestly, the removal company's price was just the start. Nobody warns you about all the extra bits – new curtains because the windows are a different size, a washing machine because the old one wouldn't fit, paint, a new sofa. By the end we'd spent nearly twice what we'd planned. The house itself is great, and the neighbours seem nice enough, but it's going to be a while before we can afford a holiday."]]},{a:5,x:"F: ‘I've started running every morning, which I'd never have done before.’ They chose the flat themselves, so G doesn't fit.",script:[["F","We chose this flat mainly because it was cheaper, and I wasn't expecting much from the area. But it's right next to a huge park, and I've started running every morning, which I'd never have done before. I've even entered a half marathon. So a move that was really just about saving money has turned out to be good for me in a completely different way."]]},{a:3,x:"D: ‘I've got to know my dad much better now that we talk as adults.’",script:[["M","I moved back in with my parents after university to save money, and I was dreading it. I thought we'd argue all the time, like when I was a teenager. But it's actually been really nice. We cook together most evenings, and I've got to know my dad much better now that we talk as adults. I'll move out eventually, but I'm in no hurry."]]},{a:7,x:"H: ‘Looking back, we should have taken more time… if we'd waited a few weeks, I think we'd have chosen differently.’",script:[["F","We saw the house on a Saturday and made an offer on the Monday, because the estate agent said there were lots of other buyers. Looking back, we should have taken more time. We never checked how noisy the road is at rush hour, and we didn't realise how far it was from the children's school. It's not terrible, but if we'd waited a few weeks, I think we'd have chosen differently."]]}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a psychologist called Dr Nadia Osei, who researches happiness.",script:[["M","My guest today is Dr Nadia Osei, a psychologist who researches happiness. Nadia, welcome."],["F","Thank you."],["M","How did you end up studying happiness? It's not an obvious career."],["F","No, it wasn't planned at all. I trained as a clinical psychologist, so I spent years working with people who were depressed or anxious. What I noticed was that when they got better, we knew very little about how to help them actually flourish, rather than just stop suffering. That gap is what pulled me into research."],["M","One of your best-known studies is about money. What did you find?"],["F","People often expect me to say that money doesn't make you happy, but that's not quite true. Having enough to live without constant worry makes a big difference. Beyond that point, though, earning more has surprisingly little effect. What seems to matter more is how you spend it. People who spend money on experiences, or on other people, report more lasting happiness than those who buy things for themselves."],["M","Is happiness something we can change, or are we born with a certain level?"],["F","Genes do play a part – some people are naturally more cheerful. But research suggests that a large proportion is influenced by our habits and choices. I'm always careful here, though, because I don't want people to think that being unhappy is their own fault. Things like illness, poverty or loneliness have a real impact, and you can't simply think your way out of those."],["M","Which habits make the biggest difference?"],["F","If I had to pick one, it would be relationships. The strongest predictor of wellbeing in almost every study is the quality of people's connections with others. It doesn't need to be a huge circle of friends – a few close relationships are enough. Exercise and sleep help too, of course, but they come second."],["M","What about social media? Does it make us unhappy?"],["F","It's more complicated than the headlines suggest. Using it to stay in touch with people you care about can be positive. The problem is passive scrolling – looking at other people's carefully edited lives and comparing yourself with them. In our studies, it wasn't the amount of time people spent online that predicted a lower mood, but what they were actually doing while they were there."],["M","Do you apply your research to your own life?"],["F","I try to, but I'm not a perfect example. I work too much, like most academics. What I have changed is that I now keep my weekends free of work, and I make a point of seeing friends every week, even when I'm busy. I used to cancel plans whenever a deadline came up. I don't do that any more."],["M","And finally, what would you say to someone who wants to feel happier?"],["F","Don't aim to be happy all the time. That's not realistic, and people who try usually end up disappointed. Feeling sad or frustrated sometimes is a normal part of life. Focus instead on doing things that feel meaningful to you, and happiness tends to follow."]],qs:[{q:"What led Nadia to start researching happiness?",o:["A colleague suggested it to her.","She was unhappy in her previous job.","She noticed a gap in what was known."],a:2,x:"‘We knew very little about how to help them actually flourish… That gap is what pulled me into research.’"},{q:"What does Nadia say about money and happiness?",o:["Money has no effect on how happy people are.","How people spend money matters more than how much they earn.","People who earn more are always happier."],a:1,x:"‘Beyond that point… earning more has surprisingly little effect. What seems to matter more is how you spend it.’"},{q:"Why is Nadia careful when she talks about changing how happy we are?",o:["She doesn't want unhappy people to blame themselves.","The research on genes is still uncertain.","She thinks people exaggerate the effect of habits."],a:0,x:"‘I don't want people to think that being unhappy is their own fault.’"},{q:"According to Nadia, what has the greatest effect on wellbeing?",o:["having close relationships","getting enough sleep","having a large group of friends"],a:0,x:"‘The strongest predictor… is the quality of people's connections… a few close relationships are enough.’ Sleep comes ‘second’."},{q:"What does Nadia say about social media?",o:["People should spend less time online.","It has been proved to damage people's mood.","What people do online matters more than how long they spend."],a:2,x:"‘It wasn't the amount of time people spent online… but what they were actually doing.’"},{q:"What change has Nadia made in her own life?",o:["She has reduced her working hours.","She no longer cancels plans with friends.","She has started exercising every week."],a:1,x:"‘I used to cancel plans whenever a deadline came up. I don't do that any more.’ She still works too much (A)."},{q:"What is Nadia's final advice?",o:["Try to stay positive in every situation.","Set clear goals for your happiness.","Accept that negative feelings are normal."],a:2,x:"‘Don't aim to be happy all the time… Feeling sad or frustrated sometimes is a normal part of life.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.listening.push({id:"l3",title:"Listening Test 3",topics:"Everyday situations · A beekeeper · Moving to a new city · A mountain photographer",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear a woman telling a friend about a film she saw.",q:"What did she think of it?",o:["The story was hard to follow.","It was too long.","The acting was disappointing."],a:1,x:"‘Three hours? They could have cut forty minutes easily.’ She praises the actors and says she had no trouble following the story.",script:[["F","Everyone said it was brilliant, and to be fair the actors were fantastic – especially the girl who played the daughter. And I didn't have any trouble following what was going on, even with all the jumps backwards and forwards in time. But honestly, three hours? They could have cut forty minutes easily. By the end I just wanted to go home."],["M","So you wouldn't recommend it?"],["F","I would, but only to people with a lot of patience."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about learning to cook.",q:"Why did he start taking cookery lessons?",o:["to save money","to impress his partner","to eat more healthily"],a:2,x:"After a check-up his cholesterol was high and he had been ‘living on ready meals for years’. He rejects the other two reasons.",script:[["M","People always assume I did it to impress my girlfriend, but she's a much better cook than me and she was happy doing it. And cooking from scratch isn't really cheaper, not with the ingredients I like. No, it was after a check-up at the doctor's. My cholesterol was high, and I realised I'd been living on ready meals for years. That was the push I needed."]]},{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about a new sports centre.",q:"What does the woman complain about?",o:["the opening hours","the price of membership","the lack of parking"],a:0,x:"‘It doesn't open until nine, which is no good if you want to swim before work.’ She thinks the price is reasonable and it's next to the car park.",script:[["F","Have you been to the new sports centre yet?"],["M","Once. It's pretty expensive, though."],["F","Is it? I thought it was reasonable for what you get – there's a pool and two gyms. And it's right next to the car park, which is great. My only problem is that it doesn't open until nine, which is no good if you want to swim before work."],["M","That's a shame."]]},{ctx:"You hear a teacher talking to a student.",q:"What is the teacher doing?",o:["encouraging the student to enter a competition","warning the student about a deadline","suggesting the student changes a story"],a:0,x:"‘I think you should send it in. You wouldn't need to change a thing.’ The closing date is a month away, so it isn't a warning.",script:[["F","Jamie, have you got a minute? I've just read your short story – the one about the lighthouse. It's really good. There's a writing competition for young people run by the local library, and the closing date's not until the end of next month, so there's plenty of time. I think you should send it in. You wouldn't need to change a thing."],["M","Really? I wasn't sure it was good enough."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man leaving a voicemail message.",q:"Why is he calling?",o:["to change an arrangement","to apologise for missing something","to ask for directions"],a:1,x:"‘I'm so sorry about last night. I completely forgot we'd arranged to have dinner.’ Arranging another evening is for later.",script:[["M","Hi Sophie, it's Dan. I'm so sorry about last night. I completely forgot we'd arranged to have dinner – I only remembered this morning when I saw your messages. I know you waited for ages. There's no excuse, really. Let me make it up to you – I'll call you later and we can sort out another evening, if you're still speaking to me!"]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about her job as a tour guide.",q:"What does she find most difficult about the job?",o:["remembering historical facts","dealing with bad weather","keeping groups together"],a:2,x:"‘What really wears me out is making sure nobody wanders off.’ Facts ‘stick in your head’ and rain doesn't bother her.",script:[["F","People think the hard part must be learning all the dates and names, but after a few weeks they just stick in your head. And rain doesn't bother me – I've got a good umbrella, and tourists expect it in London anyway. What really wears me out is making sure nobody wanders off. You turn round to point at a building, and suddenly two people have disappeared into a souvenir shop."]]},{ctx:"You hear two students talking about a presentation.",q:"What do they decide to do?",o:["practise together at the weekend","divide the work between them","ask their tutor for more time"],a:1,x:"‘I think we should just split it’ – ‘OK, that makes sense.’ The tutor has refused extensions and she is working all weekend.",script:[["M","We've only got a week. Should we ask Dr Hall for an extension?"],["F","She's already said no to everyone else. I think we should just split it – you do the history part and I'll do the statistics."],["M","OK, that makes sense. And we could meet on Saturday to run through it?"],["F","I'm working all weekend, sorry. Let's just send each other our slides."],["M","Fine. Deal."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a holiday.",q:"How did he feel about the hotel?",o:["It was better than he had expected.","It was not as described online.","It was too far from the beach."],a:0,x:"‘I wasn't expecting much. But the room was huge…’ It was five minutes from the beach, ‘like the website said’.",script:[["M","I'd booked it at the last minute, and the reviews were pretty mixed, so I wasn't expecting much. But the room was huge, the staff couldn't do enough for us, and it was exactly five minutes' walk to the beach, like the website said. Honestly, we'd happily go back."]]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a woman called Chloe Barnes talking about her work as a beekeeper.",script:[["F",`Hello everyone. I'm Chloe, and I've been keeping bees for about twelve years now. I actually started when I was working as an accountant – a friend gave me a hive as a birthday present, which I thought was a very strange gift at the time.

Today I look after sixty hives on farms around the county. A strong hive in summer can contain around fifty thousand bees, although that number drops a lot in winter.

People often ask me whether I get stung. The answer is yes, several times a week, but I'm used to it. The thing I really worry about isn't stings, it's the weather. A long, wet spring is the worst thing for bees, because they can't fly out to collect food.

Most of my honey is sold at farmers' markets, but I also supply a small bakery in the town centre, which uses it in their bread.

The flavour of honey depends completely on the flowers the bees visit. Honey from bees near lavender fields, for example, is quite different from honey made near apple orchards. My personal favourite is heather honey, which is dark and quite strong.

One thing I'd love people to understand is how important bees are for food production. Roughly a third of the food we eat depends on insects like bees for pollination.

If you want to help, you don't need to keep bees yourself. The simplest thing is to plant flowers, and to avoid chemical sprays in your garden. And if you do want to become a beekeeper, my advice is to join a local club first. You'll learn far more from experienced keepers than you ever will from a book.`]],items:[{s:"Chloe was working as an {g} when she started keeping bees.",a:["accountant"],x:"‘I actually started when I was working as an accountant.’"},{s:"Her first hive was a {g} from a friend.",a:["birthday present","present","gift","birthday gift"],x:"‘a friend gave me a hive as a birthday present’."},{s:"She now looks after {g} hives.",a:["sixty","60"],x:"‘Today I look after sixty hives.’"},{s:"In summer, a strong hive can contain about {g} bees.",a:["fifty thousand","50,000","50000","50 000"],x:"‘around fifty thousand bees’."},{s:"Chloe worries more about the {g} than about being stung.",a:["weather"],x:"‘The thing I really worry about isn't stings, it's the weather.’"},{s:"As well as selling at markets, she supplies honey to a {g}.",a:["bakery","small bakery"],x:"‘I also supply a small bakery in the town centre’."},{s:"Honey made near lavender tastes different from honey made near {g}.",a:["apple orchards","orchards"],x:"‘quite different from honey made near apple orchards’."},{s:"Chloe's favourite kind of honey is {g} honey.",a:["heather"],x:"‘My personal favourite is heather honey.’"},{s:"About {g} of our food depends on pollination by insects.",a:["a third","one third","1/3"],x:"‘Roughly a third of the food we eat depends on insects.’"},{s:"She advises new beekeepers to join a {g}.",a:["local club","club","beekeeping club"],x:"‘my advice is to join a local club first’."}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people talk about moving to a new city.",prompt:"For questions 19–23, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker says about the experience. Use the letters only once. There are three extra letters which you do not need to use.",options:["I found it hard to make friends at first.","I was surprised by how expensive everything was.","I regret not moving sooner.","I miss the slower pace of life where I grew up.","I moved because of a relationship.","I got lost more often than I expected.","I had to learn a new language quickly.","I found work more easily than I expected."],speakers:[{a:2,x:"C: ‘Why on earth did I wait so long? I'd be so much further on if I'd done it at twenty-five.’",script:[["M","I spent years talking about leaving my hometown and moving to Manchester, and I kept finding reasons not to – my job, my flat, my football team. When I finally did it, at thirty-two, everything just fell into place. I found a flat near the centre and joined a running club in my first week. My only thought now is: why on earth did I wait so long? I'd be so much further on if I'd done it at twenty-five."]]},{a:3,x:"D: ‘I find the rush exhausting… I realise how much I've missed just slowing down.’ She says the cost isn't as bad as people warned (not B).",script:[["F","I grew up in a tiny village in Wales, and everyone told me I'd love London. And in lots of ways I do – there's always something on, and the cost isn't as bad as people warned me, because I share a flat. But I find the rush exhausting. Everyone's walking fast; nobody has time for a chat in the shop. When I go home to visit, I realise how much I've missed just slowing down."]]},{a:0,x:"A: ‘What I hadn't anticipated was the loneliness… I mostly ate dinner alone.’ His job was already arranged, so H doesn't fit.",script:[["M","I moved to Edinburgh because I already had a job lined up at the university. What I hadn't anticipated was the loneliness. My colleagues were friendly at work, but everyone had their own lives in the evenings. For the first six months I mostly ate dinner alone in front of the TV. It was only when I joined a choir that things began to change."]]},{a:4,x:"E: ‘My boyfriend got a place on a medical course in Lyon… I'd never have considered living abroad if it hadn't been for him.’ She already spoke some French (not G).",script:[["F","My boyfriend got a place on a medical course in Lyon, and I didn't want us to live apart for five years, so I went too. I'd studied French at school, so I could get by, although it took a while before people stopped switching to English. The city's beautiful, and we've got a lovely group of friends now. Honestly, I'd never have considered living abroad if it hadn't been for him."]]},{a:5,x:"F: ‘for the first month I'd regularly come out on completely the wrong side of the station.’ Food was cheaper than expected (not B).",script:[["M","Tokyo is the most organised city I've ever been in, but that didn't stop me constantly ending up in the wrong place. The train stations are enormous – some have more than two hundred exits – and for the first month I'd regularly come out on completely the wrong side of the station. Everyone warned me about the prices, but actually food is cheaper than back home in London. I just wish someone had warned me about the maps!"]]}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a man called Ravi Mehta, who is a professional mountain photographer.",script:[["F","My guest today is Ravi Mehta, whose photographs of the Himalayas have appeared in magazines all over the world. Ravi, how did you get started?"],["M","Almost by accident, really. I was a keen climber long before I was a photographer. I used to take a cheap camera on expeditions just to show my family where I'd been. Then a friend who worked for a travel magazine saw some of my pictures and asked if she could use a couple. I didn't even know you could get paid for that."],["F","What's the hardest thing about taking photographs in the mountains?"],["M","People expect me to say the cold, and it's true that batteries die very quickly at high altitude – I keep spares inside my jacket. But you get used to that. The real challenge is patience. You might climb for six hours to reach a viewpoint, and then the cloud comes in and you see nothing. Sometimes I wait two or three days for the right light."],["F","Do you ever take risks to get a picture?"],["M","When I was younger, definitely. There was one time I walked out onto a ridge in bad weather because I was sure the shot would be spectacular. It was, but I also nearly fell. My guide was furious with me, and he was right. These days, no photograph is worth that. If the conditions are dangerous, I turn back."],["F","How has technology changed your work?"],["M","Drones have made a huge difference. I can now get angles that would once have needed a helicopter. But I'm a bit uncomfortable about how they're used. In some national parks there are so many drones buzzing around that they disturb the wildlife – and the other visitors. I only fly mine where it's permitted and when there's nobody else around."],["F","You also run photography trips for amateurs. What do people find most surprising?"],["M","How little the camera matters. People turn up with enormous, expensive lenses, and then they're amazed when someone with a phone takes the best picture of the day. It's all about noticing – where the light is falling, what's in the foreground. I spend the first morning of every trip without cameras at all, just looking."],["F","Is there a photograph you're proudest of?"],["M","Not one of the famous ones, actually. It's a picture of an old woman in a village in Nepal, holding a cup of tea and laughing at something my guide had said. It's not technically perfect – it's slightly out of focus – but whenever I look at it, I remember the whole day. That's what a good photograph should do."],["F","And what's next for you?"],["M","I've spent twenty years photographing the highest mountains in the world. Now I want to do the opposite. I'm starting a project about the small hills near where I grew up, in the north of England. People travel thousands of miles for a view and forget what's on their doorstep."]],qs:[{q:"Ravi became a professional photographer because",o:["he had studied photography at college.","someone asked to publish his pictures.","he wanted to show his family his climbs."],a:1,x:"A friend at a travel magazine ‘asked if she could use a couple’. Showing his family was why he took photos, not why he became a professional."},{q:"According to Ravi, the biggest challenge in his work is",o:["waiting for the right conditions.","keeping his equipment working in the cold.","carrying heavy equipment to viewpoints."],a:0,x:"‘The real challenge is patience… Sometimes I wait two or three days for the right light.’"},{q:"What does Ravi say about taking risks?",o:["He still thinks some risks are worth taking.","He now regards an earlier decision as a mistake.","His guide encouraged him to be more adventurous."],a:1,x:"He nearly fell; ‘My guide was furious with me, and he was right. These days, no photograph is worth that.’"},{q:"How does Ravi feel about drones?",o:["They are too expensive for most photographers.","They have made his work less interesting.","They can cause problems when used carelessly."],a:2,x:"Too many drones ‘disturb the wildlife – and the other visitors’."},{q:"What surprises people on Ravi's photography trips?",o:["Expensive equipment is not essential.","They have to get up very early.","The landscapes are very varied."],a:0,x:"‘How little the camera matters… someone with a phone takes the best picture of the day.’"},{q:"Why is Ravi proudest of the photo of the old woman?",o:["It was technically the hardest shot to take.","It brings back strong memories.","It made him well known."],a:1,x:"‘whenever I look at it, I remember the whole day’ – it isn't technically perfect or famous."},{q:"What is Ravi's next project?",o:["photographing mountains he has never climbed","returning to the Himalayas with new equipment","taking pictures of places near where he grew up"],a:2,x:"‘a project about the small hills near where I grew up’."}]}}}),LZ.data.listening.push({id:"l4",title:"Listening Test 4",topics:"Everyday situations · A city beekeeper · Why people learned to cook · A mountain rescue volunteer",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a bicycle she has bought.",q:"What does she say about it?",o:["It was cheaper than she expected.","It is heavier than her old one.","It needed repairing straight away."],a:1,x:"‘the frame weighs a good deal more than my old one’. The price was ‘more than I meant to spend’ and nothing needed repairing – the brakes were ‘adjusted before I collected it’.",script:[["F","I finally replaced that old bike of mine. It was more than I meant to spend, but the shop adjusted the brakes and everything before I collected it, so it was ready to ride. The only thing I hadn't thought about is that the frame weighs a good deal more than my old one. Getting it up the stairs to the flat is quite a workout. On the road you don't notice at all, mind you."]]},{ctx:"You hear two colleagues talking about a meeting.",q:"What do they agree about it?",o:["It started too late.","It covered too many topics.","It should have been an email."],a:1,x:"‘we jumped from budgets to the website to the summer party’ and he agrees ‘far too much for an hour’. Starting late is mentioned but dismissed; the email idea is the woman's joke, which he doesn't accept.",script:[["M","Well, that was two hours of my life."],["F","It only overran by ten minutes, to be fair. What got me was that we jumped from budgets to the website to the summer party."],["M","You're right, that was the problem – far too much for an hour. Nobody could follow it."],["F","It could all have been an email."],["M","Some of it, maybe, but the budget bit did need talking through."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man leaving a message about a delivery.",q:"What is he asking the company to do?",o:["deliver the item on a different day","leave the item with a neighbour","refund the delivery charge"],a:0,x:"‘could you change it to Thursday?’ He says he doesn't want it left next door, and he doesn't mention money.",script:[["M","Hello, this is Adam Price, order 4-4-1-9. You're due to deliver my table tomorrow, but I've just been told I have to be at work all day. I'd rather you didn't leave it next door, because it's far too big for their hallway. So could you change it to Thursday? I'm at home all day Thursday. If that's a problem, give me a ring."]]},{ctx:"You hear a teacher talking to a class about a trip.",q:"What is she mainly doing?",o:["reminding them what to bring","explaining why the plan has changed","telling them how to behave"],a:1,x:"‘the coach company let us down, so we're going by train instead’ – the whole message explains the change. She mentions lunch and behaviour only in passing.",script:[["F","Before you go, a quick word about Friday. The coach company let us down, so we're going by train instead. That means meeting at the station at half past eight, not at school. Bring a packed lunch as before, and obviously behave yourselves on the train – but the main thing to remember is the new meeting place. Tell your parents tonight."]]},{ctx:"You hear a young man talking about his first week in a new job.",q:"How does he feel now?",o:["confident about the work itself","worried about the hours","disappointed by his colleagues"],a:0,x:"‘the actual work I can do – that bit I'm not worried about’. The hours are ‘fine’ and the team ‘couldn't have been friendlier’.",script:[["M","Honestly, the first two days I went home with a headache – so many names, so many systems. The team couldn't have been friendlier, though, and the hours are fine, better than my last place. By Thursday something clicked. The actual work I can do – that bit I'm not worried about. It's just learning where everything is."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a film she has seen.",q:"What does she praise about it?",o:["the acting","the music","the photography"],a:2,x:"‘every single shot looked like a painting’. She calls the acting ‘solid’ (not praise) and says the music was ‘far too loud’.",script:[["F","I wasn't expecting much, but every single shot looked like a painting – the light on the water, the empty streets. I could have watched it with the sound off, actually, because the music was far too loud in places. The acting was solid, nothing more, and the story is one you've seen before. But visually, it's the best thing I've seen this year."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man and a woman talking about a holiday flat they have rented.",q:"What problem do they have with it?",o:["It is not as clean as they expected.","It is further from the beach than advertised.","It is noisier than they expected."],a:2,x:"‘the bar underneath doesn't close until two’. The flat was ‘spotless’ and the walk to the beach is ‘exactly as described’.",script:[["F","It's spotless, I'll give them that."],["M","And the beach really is five minutes, exactly as described."],["F","So why do you look so miserable?"],["M","Because the bar underneath doesn't close until two. I didn't sleep at all."],["F","Ah. I did wonder what that thumping was."]]},{ctx:"You hear a sports coach talking to a player.",q:"What is the coach doing?",o:["apologising for a decision","encouraging the player before a match","explaining why the player is not in the team"],a:2,x:"‘I'm leaving you out on Saturday’ and the reasons follow. He is positive about the future but he is not apologising.",script:[["M","Sit down a minute. I'm leaving you out on Saturday. Before you say anything – it's not about your attitude, which has been first class. You've played nine games in a row, and in the last two you've looked exhausted. I'd rather rest you now than lose you for a month. You'll be back for the cup game, and you'll be fresh."]]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a woman called Rosie Hart talking about keeping bees in a city.",script:[["F",`Good evening, everyone. My name's Rosie Hart, and I've been keeping bees here in the middle of the city for eleven years. People always ask how I started, and the honest answer is that I didn't choose it – my sister bought me a beekeeping course for my thirtieth birthday. I think she expected me to go once and forget about it. Instead I came home and ordered my first hive that same week.

Now, where do you put a hive when you live in a flat? My hives are on the roof of a library, which the council agreed to after about a year of polite letters. It's a wonderful spot: the bees fly off over the park and nobody below has any idea they're there.

The numbers surprise people. In the middle of summer, a single hive can hold up to fifty thousand bees. That's one box, one queen, and fifty thousand of her daughters. They'll travel up to five kilometres to find flowers, which means my bees visit gardens I've never even seen.

Everyone asks about equipment. You need a suit and gloves, of course, but the most important tool is a smoker. A little cool smoke at the entrance and the whole colony calms down – it's the difference between a pleasant afternoon and a very short one.

Beginners always worry about being stung. I won't pretend it doesn't happen, but after a while you barely notice. The thing nobody warns you about is the weight. A full box of honey can be twenty-five kilos, and you're lifting it above your head, on a roof, in a suit, in August.

City honey is better than most people expect. Mine tastes of lime, because the streets around the library are planted with lime trees, and for three weeks in July that's all the bees are interested in. Every neighbourhood tastes different.

In winter the bees don't hibernate, which is a common misunderstanding. They eat the honey they've stored and form a cluster, shivering their wing muscles to keep the queen at about thirty-five degrees. My job is simply to leave them enough food and not to keep opening the hive to have a look.

I sell what's left at a market on Saturdays. I never planned to sell anything, but eleven years of jars adds up, and it pays for the equipment.

If you're tempted, my advice is this: before you buy a single thing, join a local association. Someone experienced standing next to you at your first inspection is worth more than any book, and most associations will lend you a suit for the summer.`]],items:[{s:"Rosie was given a beekeeping {g} by her sister for her thirtieth birthday.",a:["course"],x:"‘my sister bought me a beekeeping course for my thirtieth birthday’"},{s:"Rosie keeps her hives on the roof of a {g}.",a:["library"],x:"‘My hives are on the roof of a library’"},{s:"In summer one hive can hold as many as {g} bees.",a:["fifty thousand","50,000","50000"],x:"‘a single hive can hold up to fifty thousand bees’"},{s:"Rosie's bees will fly up to {g} kilometres to find flowers.",a:["five","5"],x:"‘They'll travel up to five kilometres to find flowers’"},{s:"Rosie says the most important piece of equipment is a {g}.",a:["smoker"],x:"‘the most important tool is a smoker’ – the suit and gloves are mentioned first as distractors."},{s:"Rosie warns that beginners are not prepared for the {g} of a full box of honey.",a:["weight"],x:"‘The thing nobody warns you about is the weight.’"},{s:"Rosie's honey tastes of {g} because of the trees near the library.",a:["lime"],x:"‘Mine tastes of lime, because the streets around the library are planted with lime trees’"},{s:"In winter the bees eat their honey and form a {g} to keep the queen warm.",a:["cluster"],x:"‘They eat the honey they've stored and form a cluster’"},{s:"Rosie sells her honey at a {g} on Saturdays.",a:["market"],x:"‘I sell what's left at a market on Saturdays.’"},{s:"Rosie advises beginners to join a local {g} before buying anything.",a:["association"],x:"‘before you buy a single thing, join a local association’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people are talking about learning to cook.",prompt:"For questions 19–23, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker says about learning to cook. Use the letters only once. There are three extra letters which you do not need to use.",options:["I wanted to eat more healthily.","I needed to spend less money.","I was taught by an older relative.","I wanted to impress somebody.","I had to cook for somebody who was ill.","I started after a holiday abroad.","I wanted a hobby away from screens.","I learned because my job required it."],speakers:[{a:5,x:"F: two weeks in Italy and a cookery afternoon there – ‘I came home and started trying to make those things myself.’ Money is mentioned but as a result, not a reason (B).",script:[["F","Before that holiday I lived on toast, honestly. We spent two weeks in a small town in Italy, and the woman who owned the flat we rented spent an afternoon showing us how she made pasta – by hand, no machine. I came home and started trying to make those things myself. It's saved me a fortune since, but that wasn't why I started. It was that afternoon."]]},{a:6,x:"G: he stares at screens all day and wanted ‘something that used my hands and had nothing to do with a screen’. Health is a side effect (A).",script:[["M","I stare at three monitors for nine hours a day, and by the evening I'd be scrolling on my phone as well. I wanted something that used my hands and had nothing to do with a screen. Cooking turned out to be perfect – you can't check your email with flour all over your fingers. I eat better now too, which my doctor is pleased about, but that came later."]]},{a:4,x:"E: his mother came out of hospital and couldn't stand for long, so he cooked for her.",script:[["M","When Mum came out of hospital, she couldn't stand up for more than a few minutes, and someone had to feed the pair of us. My sister lives two hours away, so it was me. I started with soup, because it's hard to ruin, and worked up from there. By the time she was better I'd got quite good, and I've never stopped."]]},{a:1,x:"B: rent went up and takeaways were ‘the first thing to go’.",script:[["F","My rent went up by a hundred and fifty pounds last year, and something had to give. The takeaways were the first thing to go – I worked out I was spending forty pounds a week on food I hadn't even chosen properly. So I learned to cook about eight things really well, and I make them on repeat. It's not exciting, but my bank balance is much happier."]]},{a:3,x:"D: she invited someone she liked to dinner and ‘couldn't very well serve beans on toast’. Her grandmother is mentioned but as the source of a recipe, not the reason (C).",script:[["F","There was someone at work I rather liked, and in a moment of confidence I invited him round for dinner. Then I realised I couldn't very well serve beans on toast. I rang my grandmother in a panic and she talked me through her chicken recipe on the phone, twice. It went well – both the dinner and, for a while, the man. And I've cooked ever since."]]}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a man called Dan Whitlock, who is a volunteer with a mountain rescue team.",script:[["F","My guest today is Dan Whitlock, who spends his weekends – and quite a few nights – rescuing people from the mountains. Dan, how did you get involved?"],["M","People assume something dramatic happened to me, but it didn't. I'd been walking in the hills for years quite safely. It was seeing the team at work one afternoon – they came past us carrying someone down, and they were so calm and so organised. I thought, I'd like to be able to do that. I applied the following week."],["F","What was the training like?"],["M","Long. Eighteen months before I was allowed on a call-out on my own. I expected the ropes and the first aid to be the hard part, and they were demanding, certainly. But what I hadn't imagined was how much navigation there'd be. You're finding a specific rock in the dark, in cloud, with a map and a compass, because you can't rely on a phone signal. That took me the longest to get right."],["F","And the equipment – that must cost a fortune."],["M","It does, and that's the part the public doesn't see. We're a charity; we get no government funding at all. A stretcher is three thousand pounds, a radio set more. People are very generous when we rattle a tin at the supermarket, but I do wish they knew the ambulance service doesn't pay for us."],["F","What do most of your call-outs involve?"],["M","Not what you'd think. Broken ankles, mostly – somebody slips on wet rock a mile from the car park. Real climbing accidents are rare. The single biggest cause of trouble, though, is simply running out of daylight. People set off at two in the afternoon in November and are surprised when it's dark at four."],["F","Does it annoy you, going out to people who weren't prepared?"],["M","I'm asked that a lot, and the honest answer is no. Anyone can misjudge a day out – I have. What does frustrate me is when people don't ring us because they're embarrassed, and then we're looking for them at midnight in the rain instead of at six o'clock in the light. Call early. Nobody will tell you off."],["F","How does your family feel about it?"],["M","My wife was worried at the start, and I understand why. What helped was when she came to an open day and saw the systems – how careful it all is. She's on the fundraising committee now. My daughter, who's nine, thinks it's completely normal that Dad leaves halfway through Sunday lunch."],["F","And what next?"],["M","I've started training as a search dog handler, which takes another two years. The dogs find people in minutes that twenty of us would take hours to find. If I'm honest, though, the real reason is that I've always wanted an excuse to spend that much time with a dog."]],qs:[{q:"Why did Dan join the mountain rescue team?",o:["He had been in danger in the mountains himself.","He was impressed when he watched the team working.","A member of the team encouraged him to apply."],a:1,x:"‘It was seeing the team at work one afternoon… I thought, I'd like to be able to do that.’ He says nothing dramatic happened to him."},{q:"What did Dan find most difficult during the training?",o:["the navigation","the rope work","the first aid"],a:0,x:"The ropes and first aid were ‘demanding’, but navigation ‘took me the longest to get right’."},{q:"What does Dan want the public to understand about the equipment?",o:["that donated money is spent carefully","that the team is not paid for by the state","that modern equipment is safer"],a:1,x:"‘We're a charity; we get no government funding at all… the ambulance service doesn't pay for us.’"},{q:"According to Dan, what causes most problems in the mountains?",o:["climbing accidents","sudden changes in the weather","people running out of daylight"],a:2,x:"‘The single biggest cause of trouble, though, is simply running out of daylight.’ Broken ankles are the most common injury, but daylight causes the trouble."},{q:"How does Dan feel about people who set off unprepared?",o:["He accepts that anyone can make a mistake.","He thinks they should pay for the rescue.","He believes they should be given advice at the car park."],a:0,x:"‘Anyone can misjudge a day out – I have.’ What frustrates him is people waiting too long to call."},{q:"What does Dan say about his family?",o:["His wife still finds it difficult.","His wife now helps the team.","His daughter worries when he leaves."],a:1,x:"‘She's on the fundraising committee now.’ His daughter thinks it is normal."},{q:"Why is Dan training as a dog handler?",o:["because dogs search far more quickly","because the team is short of handlers","mainly because he wants to work with a dog"],a:2,x:"He gives the speed as the official reason, then: ‘If I'm honest, though, the real reason is that I've always wanted an excuse to spend that much time with a dog.’"}]}}});
